Add a built-in ${DATE} field: the date when the label is printed.

Text objects and barcode/QR data can now contain ${DATE}.  It is evaluated
each time a label is rendered -- printing, PDF export, print preview and
glabels-batch-qt -- so it always shows the current date at print time.

  ${DATE}                      today, in the system's short date format
  ${DATE:@yyyy-MM-dd}          custom Qt date/time format, e.g. @dd/MM/yyyy,
                               @d MMM yyyy, @yyyy-MM-dd hh:mm.  Must be the last
                               modifier; write a literal '}' as \}
  ${DATE:+30}  ${DATE:-1}      offset in days (e.g. an expiry date)
  ${DATE:+2w} ${DATE:+6m} ${DATE:+1y}   ...or weeks, months, years
  ${DATE:+30:@yyyy-MM-dd}      modifiers combine; also with :n and :%...

The "Insert substitution field" button for text and barcodes gains a
"Date when printed" section with ready-made formats, each showing what it
gives today.  The image and colour field buttons are unchanged.

A merge field or user variable named DATE (non-empty) still takes precedence,
so existing labels that use such a field are unaffected.  Field names are
case-sensitive: ${date} is not the built-in field.

The editor canvas shows "${DATE}" as typed, like any other field; the
printed date shows in the print preview and output.

Labels using ${DATE} open in unpatched gLabels, but the field renders empty
there (or as its :=default).

Covered by new dateParse/dateEvaluation cases in TestSubstitutionField.
glabels_C.ts is deliberately not touched; new strings show in English until
the maintainer next runs lupdate.

Removable if upstream adds an equivalent date field.

